  "solution": {
    "approach": "For a pair of vertices a < b the unique path between them is a clamp path iff the smallest vertex number on the path equals a and the largest equals b.  We preprocess the tree for LCA queries using binary lifting.  For every 2^k ancestor we also store the minimum and maximum vertex number on the upward segment.  Then for each ordered pair (a,b) we obtain the LCA, combine the minima and maxima on the two upward segments (a → LCA and b → LCA) and check whether min == a and max == b.  All such pairs are counted.  The preprocessing takes O(N log N) time and O(N log N) memory.  The double loop over all pairs costs O(N^2 log N) which is fast enough for N ≤ 2000.",
    "timeComplexity": "O(N log N + N^2 log N) – preprocessing O(N log N), each of the O(N^2) pairs needs O(log N) to obtain min/max on the path.",
    "spaceComplexity": "O(N log N) – binary lifting tables for parent, min and max.",
    "code": {
